BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Film Screening: Raging Bull — Berkeley/San Francisco
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-08:00":20261121T183000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-08:00":20261121T193000
DTSTAMP:20261121T192054Z
UID:89e95cfac5c75cfb9b68fe1407a8@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Films\,Berkeley/San Francisco\,United States
DESCRIPTION:Martin Scorsese and Robert De Niro make the biography of boxer
  Jake LaMotta into a visceral meditation on existential suffering. Eschewi
 ng the romance associated with the sport\, Raging Bull shows the struggle 
 of an athlete whose willful determination to succeed on his own terms take
 s its toll on all those around him—friends and adversaries in equal meas
 ure—before he is undermined by self-loathing. Every punch is framed\, li
 t\, and cut for maximum impact\, with black-and-white cinematography inspi
 red by James Wong Howe. “A fusion of Hollywood genre with personal visio
 n … closer to poetry than pulp. Its sculptural weight can only be apprec
 iated on the big screen” (Amy Taubin\, Village Voice ).\nCategory: Films
 \nOriginal listing: https://events.berkeley.edu/BAMPFA/event/331159-film-s
 creening-raging-bull
LOCATION:BAMPFA\, BAMPFA
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://events.berkeley.edu/BAMPFA/event/331159-film-screening-raging-
 bull
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:berkeley-arts
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jason Moran and the Harlem Hell Fighters: James Reese Europe and t
 he Absence of Ruin — Berkeley/San Francisco
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-08:00":20261121T190000
DTSTAMP:20261121T192054Z
UID:03c7189ddb6cf321fb457ebb0df3@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Performing Arts\,Berkeley/San Francisco\,United
  States
DESCRIPTION:The Absence of Ruin is jazz pianist and composer Jason Moran
 ’s multidisciplinary meditation on the life and legacy of James Reese Eu
 rope\, the underrecognized Black American composer and bandleader who land
 ed in France with the Harlem Hellfighters on New Year’s Day 1918. The ce
 lebrated all-Black unit helped break the stalemate on the Western Front du
 ring the First World War\, while composer Europe’s finely honed military
  music ensemble popularized the new spirit of jazz in a war-torn France fa
 scinated with Black culture. Moran’s layered work explores the idea of m
 emory in Black culture\, augmenting his long-standing Bandwagon bandmates
 ––bassist Tarus Mateen and drummer Nasheet Waits––with a sev\nCate
 gory: Performing Arts\nOriginal listing: https://events.berkeley.edu/calpe
 rfs/event/323610-jason-moran-and-the-harlem-hell-fighters-james
LOCATION:101 Zellerbach Hall\, Berkeley\, CA 94720\, 101 Zellerbach Hall\,
  Berkeley\, CA 94720
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://events.berkeley.edu/calperfs/event/323610-jason-moran-and-the-
 harlem-hell-fighters-james
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:berkeley-arts
END:VEVENT
